David Damerell wrote on 21 Dec 2003 14:03:34 +0000 (GMT):
> Stanislav Traykov  <news@tortoises.org> wrote:
>>While there's nothing wrong with it, the player cannot *deduce* that
>>it's random and happens the way he thinks it happens (there'd always
>>be a non-0 probability that the pseudorandom numbers just happened to
>>satisfy the players hypothesis). A price-IDing table is on the other
>>hand 100% in-game-deducible.

> Of course this is not true. Every observed price may be equally explained
> by the theory that any price is randomly between 0 and 2,000 zorkmids.
> There is a non-zero probability that this scheme would produce the
> observed data.

Well, I corrected myself in the next post. I provided a too strong
"anti-deducing argument" which killed off mine as well :-)

It's clear that nothing is deducible w/o looking at the code (and even
in this case, as in any case, it could be that the world is just
experiencing a random lapse of chaos towards you and acts according to
your theory of it). Nevertheless, Mr. Wayne chose to contrast the
knowledge that items don't get cursed independently but instead in a
way that allows curse-catchers to be effective with the knowledge that
buy prices are constant for a particular Cha (except for the [also
constant] % off which occurs from time to time). He said that "[i]t
requires no out-of-game knowledge (unlike, say, detailed price
id-ing)".

This particular contrast looked interesting because: if we ignore
other game mechanics and have two players, A and B, the former trying
to deduce the first statement (knowledge of that particular aspect of
the curse algorithm) and the latter trying to deduce the second
(knowledge of price stability [and thus table deducibility]) it may
well turn out that the contrast was in the wrong direction: that
player A progresses faster than player B towards 100% probability of
his claim (~B will be majorant of ~A, both going towards 0), assuming
best possible scenarios for each player.

Honestly, after thinking about "best possible scenarios", I'm not so
sure which will go faster. In any case it seems to be not trivial, as
(IMO) Mr. Wayne made it sound in his comparison.

